  <page>
    <title>International Finance Centre</title>
    <id>185121</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41861196</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-02T05:11:40Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Buckyboy314</username>
        <id>1004080</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Ifc2.jpg|thumb|120px|Two ifc]]

'''International Finance Centre''' (abbr. '''IFC''', [[brand]]ed as &quot;ifc&quot;; [[Traditional Chinese character|Traditional Chinese]]: 國際金融中心; [[Simplified Chinese character|Simplified Chinese]]: 国际金融中心; [[Jyutping]]: gwok3 zai3 gam1 jung4 zung1 sam1; [[pinyin]]: Guójìjīnróngzhōngxīn) is an integrated commercial development on the waterfront of [[Hong Kong]]'s [[Central, Hong Kong|Central District]] ({{coor dms|22|17|6.82|N|114|9|33.45|E|}}).  A prominent landmark on [[Hong Kong Island]], it consists of three [[skyscraper]]s and the ifc mall. The [[Airport Express (MTR)|Airport Express]] [[Hong Kong Station (MTR)|Hong Kong Station]] is directly beneath it.


==One International Finance Centre==
Also known as 1IFC and branded as &quot;One ifc&quot;, One International Finance Centre was completed in [[1998]] and opened in [[1999]]. It is [[1 E2 m|180 m]] tall, has 39 storeys and four trading floors, 18 high speed passenger lifts in 4 zones, and comprises 784,000 square feet (72,800&amp;nbsp;m&amp;sup2;). The building has attracted global financial institutions as its tenants, making One ifc a key business destination in Hong Kong. The building currently accommodates approximately 5,000 people.

==Two International Finance Centre==
[[Image:StarFerry-TST.png|thumb|300px|The 1ifc (right) and the 2ifc (left) as seen from the [[Star Ferry]] Pier at [[Tsim Sha Tsui]] on the [[Kowloon Peninsula|opposite coast]] of the [[Victoria Harbour]].]] 

Also known also as 2IFC and branded as &quot;Two ifc&quot;, Two International Finance Centre was completed in [[2003]]. Designed by [[César Pelli]], this 415 m tall building is currently Hong Kong's tallest, with 88 storeys (an extremely lucky number in Cantonese culture) and 22 high-ceiling trading floors. The highrise is designed to accommodate financial firms. For example, the [[Hong Kong Monetary Authority]] (HKMA) is located at the 55th floor. It is equipped with state of the art telecommunications, raised floors for flexible cabling management, and nearly column-free floor plans. The building expects to accommodate up to 15,000 people.

It should be noted that, however, the 88 storeys do not comprise of an actual number of 88 storeys. Some of the &quot;[[taboo]] floors&quot; like 14 and 24, which sounds like ''Must die'' and ''Easy to die'' in [[Cantonese (linguistics)|Cantonese]] respectively, are omitted.

The top floor of Two ifc is slightly higher than the city's landmark [[Victoria Peak|the Peak]] viewing gallery in Hong Kong. It is the third tallest building in the [[Greater China|Greater China region]] and the 6th tallest office building in the world after:
* [[Taipei 101]] in [[Taipei]], [[Taiwan]]
* the [[Petronas Twin Towers]] in [[Kuala Lumpur]], [[Malaysia]]
* the [[Sears Tower]] in [[Chicago]], [[United States|USA]]
* the [[Jin Mao Tower]] in [[Shanghai]], [[China]] [http://www.emporis.com/en/bu/sk/st/tp/wo/]
The rankings are based on [[structural height]]s; by roof height only Taipei 101 and Sears Tower exceed it.

Tenants of the building include, but are not limited to: [[Hong Kong Monetary Authority]], [[Warburg Pincus]], [[Nomura Group]], [[UBS AG]], [[Ernst &amp; Young]], [[State Street Corporation]], [[Financial Times]] and [[Lehman Brothers]]. 

'''Four Seasons Hotel Hong Kong''' was completed in [[2005]].  The 40-storey hotel is the first ''[[Four Seasons Hotel|Four Seasons]]'' in Hong Kong.

The '''ifc mall''' features a number of top [[fashion]], health &amp; skin care, [[jewelry]] &amp; accessories shops, [[restaurant]]s, and a [[movie theater|cinema]]. The ifc mall houses over 200 different brands. It also features Hong Kong's third [[c!ty'super]] store, a gourmet food grocery.
-----------------------------------------------
&lt;gallery&gt;
Image:Ifc logo.jpg|The ifc logo on the building facade.&lt;/td&gt;
Image:Ifc interior.jpg|Various shops and a cinema inside the ifc mall.
Image:Ifc fashion show.jpg|A fashion show performed in ifc.
Image:Ifc office.jpg|The office lobby in Two ifc.
&lt;/gallery&gt;
-----------------------------------------------

==Notes on two ifc==
[[Image:HK-2IFC-sj.jpg|thumb|200px|Two ifc at night]]
* It is one of relatively few buildings in the world equipped with [[double-deck elevator]]s.  
* The Hong Kong Monetary Authority bought several floors in the top portion of the tower for USD 0.48 bn.  
* [[Financial Times]], [[HSBC]], and [[Cathay Pacific]] put up an advertisement on the facade from October to November of 2003 that stretched more than 50 storeys, covering an area of 0.2 million square ft (19,000 m²) and a length of 230 m; making it the world's largest advertisement ever put on skyscraper.  
* This is the tallest building in Hong Kong, surpassing the [[Central Plaza, Hong Kong|Central Plaza]].  
* Two ifc's reign as Hong Kong's tallest will not be long, as [[Union Square Phase 7]] is under construction and will claim the title in [[2007]].  
* More than 3,500 workers from various places around the world worked at the construction site during the peak construction period.  
* The Two ifc building was featured in the [[Hollywood]] movie ''[[Tomb Raider: The Cradle of Life]]''.  
* The 88th floor of the tower contains the office of the Chief Executive of the HK Monetary Authority. It is served by an individual lift.
* The tower is attached to the second phase of the ifc mall.
* There is no public [[Art gallery|viewing gallery]] in Two ifc.
* An exhibition area and a library of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority Information Centre occupy the 55th floor and are open to the public during office hours. 
* The number of storeys (88) has in this case a special significance, as the number [[8 (number)|eight]] (八, formal writing 捌, [[pinyin]] bā) is considered a [[lucky number]] in [[Chinese culture]] because it sounds like the word &quot;prosperity&quot; (發 [[pinyin]]: fā).
*Some locals as well as tourists criticised that the tower obstructed the famous view of Victoria Harbour from the Peak.
* Developed by a consortium lead by [[Sun Hung Kai Properties]] &amp; [[MTR Corp]].
-----------------------------------------------
&lt;gallery&gt;
Image:Ifc hong kong station.jpg|The Hong Kong Station in ifc.
Image:Ifc way to pier.jpg|A flyover from ifc to the outlying island piers. (Small) The [[Lantau Island]] Pier.
Image:Ifc in town check in.jpg|Airline passengers can check in at Hong Kong Station, next to the ifc.
Image:Ifc hotel.jpg|Four Seasons Hotel and Suites under construction.
Image:Ifc hong kong station ad.jpg|A [[Hong Kong (MTR)|station]] of the [[Airport Express (MTR)|Airport Express]] is beneath ifc.
&lt;/gallery&gt;

==See also==
* [[List of the world's tallest structures]]
* [[List of buildings and structures in Hong Kong]]

==External links==
{{commons|Category:International Finance Centre|International Finance Centre}}
*[http://maps.google.com/maps?ll=22.285734,114.158088&amp;spn=0.003761,0.005759&amp;t=k&amp;hl=en Satellite image of ifc by Google Maps]
*[http://www.ifc.com.hk/ ifc's website]
*[http://www.emporis.com/en/wm/bu/?id=100614 Emporis - Two International Finance Centre ]
*[http://www.fourseasons.com/hongkong/ Four Seasons Hotel Hong Kong]
