      </contributor>
      <comment>worms -&gt; earthworms</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Taxobox
| color = pink
| name = Tickell's Thrush
| regnum = [[Animal]]ia
| phylum = [[Chordate|Chordata]]
| classis = [[bird|Aves]]
| ordo = [[Passeriformes]]
| familia = [[Turdidae]]
| genus = '''''[[Turdus]]'''''
| species = '''''T. unicolor'''''
| binomial = ''Turdus unicolor''
| binomial_authority = [[Samuel Tickell|Tickell]], [[1833]]
}}

The '''Tickell's Thrush''' (''Turdus unicolor'') is a [[passerine]] [[bird]] in the [[thrush (bird)|thrush]] family Turdidae.

It is common in open forest in the [[Himalaya]]. Populations move further south in [[India]] in winter.

Tickell's Thrushes are [[omnivorous]], eating a wide range of [[insect]]s, [[earthworm]]s and [[Berry|berries]]. They nest in bushes or similar. They do not form flocks.

Males of this small thrush have uniform blue-grey upperparts, and a whitish belly and vent. Females and young birds have browner upperparts.

[[Category:Turdidae]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Orange-headed Thrush</title>
    <id>185151</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40630042</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-21T23:07:44Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Samsara</username>
        <id>19527</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>worms -&gt; earthworms</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Taxobox
| color = pink
| name = Orange-headed Thrush
| image = orange-headed.thrush.750pix.jpg
| image_width = 250px
| regnum = [[Animal]]ia
| phylum = [[Chordate|Chordata]]
| classis = [[bird|Aves]]
| ordo = [[Passeriformes]]
| familia = [[Turdidae]]
| genus = '''''[[Zoothera]]'''''
| species = '''''Z. citrina'''''
| binomial = ''Zoothera citrina''
| binomial_authority = ([[John Latham (ornithologist)|Latham]], [[1790]])
}}

The '''Orange-headed Thrush''' (''Zoothera citrina'') is a member of the [[thrush (bird)|thrush]] family, Turdidae.

It is common in well-wooded areas of [[India]], [[China]] and southeast [[Asia]].  Most populations are resident. The species shows a preference for shady damp areas, and like many ''[[Zoothera]]'' thrushes, can be quite secretive.

Orange-headed Thrushes are [[omnivore|omnivorous]], eating a wide range of [[insect]]s, [[earthworm]]s and fruit. They nest in trees. They do not form flocks.

Males of this small thrush have uniform grey upperparts, and orange head and underparts. Females and young birds have browner upperparts.

The race ''Zoothera citrina cyanotus'' is a resident in the hills of southern India and the [[Western Ghats]]. They have a white throat and two black stripes running below the eyes. This race is known locally as the '''White-throated Ground Thrush'''.</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Chat (bird)</title>
    <id>185152</id>
    <revision>
      <id>37373336</id>
