      <comment>[[WP:AWB|AWB assisted]] re-categorisation per [[WP:CFD|CFD]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Lucius Livius Andronicus''' ([[280]]/[[260 BCE]]?&amp;ndash;[[200 BCE]]?), was a [[Greco-Roman]] [[dramatist]] and [[epic poetry|epic poet]] who produced the first Roman dramatic work and translated many [[Greek language|Greek]] works into [[Latin language|Latin]]. He is regarded as the father of Roman drama and epic poetry.

Andronicus was probably born in the Greek colony of [[Tarentum]] (now [[Taranto]], [[Italy]]), and was made a slave to a [[Livius|Livian]] noble. After being freed and adopting the name of his former master, Andronicus taught and produced for the uncultured Romans their first formal play, a translation of a Greek drama, in [[240 BCE]]. His most important work is ''Odysseia'', a Latin version of [[Homer]]'s [[Odyssey]], written in [[saturnian (poetry)|Saturnian verses]]. He is also quoted as producing various other poems.
{{ancient-Rome-bio-stub}}
