  <page>
    <title>Chaconne</title>
    <id>185229</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35868589</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-19T22:27:31Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>69.164.121.206</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[music]] a '''chaconne''' is a [[musical form]].

Originally a kind of [[dance]] in a slow 3/4 [[time signature|time]] which first emerged in the [[16th century]] and which is probably of [[Spain|Spanish]] provenance, the word was later applied to any work in 3/4 consisting of a set of variations over a never harmonically changing bass (a [[ground bass]]). One of the best known examples is the chaconne from the [[Solo Violin Partita No. 2 (Bach)|Violin Partita in D minor]] by [[Johann Sebastian Bach]] (the bass line is not always present in this work, but it is strongly implied). Bach's ''[[Goldberg Variations]]'' are also frequently reckoned to be a chaconne, although Bach did not explicitly label them as such. After the [[baroque music|baroque]] period, the chaconne form was not often used, though the ''32 Variations in C minor'' by [[Ludwig van Beethoven]] belong to the form.

The chaconne is almost identical with the [[passacaglia]], except that in the passacaglia the repeated theme is not always in the bass.  Like the term ''passacaglia'', the chaconne need not be in 3/4 time (note the ''Chaconne'' from [[George Frideric Handel|Handel]]'s [[G minor]] [[clavier]] [[suite]]).  It normally consists of a more-or-less fixed [[chord progression]] of usually 8 or 16 [[bar (music)|bars]] that is repeated in tandem throughout most of the piece.  A common chord progression is the &quot;[[circle of fifths]]&quot; progression, especially in a [[minor key]], but others progressions are also often used.

==Examples of Chaconnes==
*[[Johann Sebastian Bach]]: ''Chaconne'' from [[Solo Violin Partita No. 2 (Bach)|''Partita #2 in D minor for Solo Violin'']]
*[[Johann Pachelbel]]: [[Pachelbel's Canon|''Canon in D'']] (see that article for more examples using the chord progression of Pachelbel's Canon)
*[[George Frideric Handel]]: ''Chaconne'' from [[Suite]] in [[G minor]] for [[clavier]]
*[[Michiru Yamane]]: ''Chaconne in C minor'' from the soundtrack of [[Castlevania: Symphony of the Night|Demon Castle Dracula X: Nocturne in the Moonlight]] (note: this piece bears striking resemblances to the Passacaglia of J. S. Bach's [[Passacaglia and Fugue in C Minor]] for [[organ (music)|organ]])
*[[Philip Glass]]: Symphony No.3, third, slow movement (1995) 
*[[Eagles]]: ''[[Hotel California]]''
*[[Gloria Gaynor]], and later, others: ''[[I Will Survive]]''

==External links==
*[http://maitre.physik.uni-kl.de/~monerjan/ A transcription of the Urtext of Bach's Chaconne]

*[http://www.easybyte.org Easybyte] - free easy piano transcription of &quot;Chaconne BWV 1004 / Second Theme Excerpt&quot; by J. S. Bach

[[de:Chaconne]] 
[[fr:Chaconne]] 
[[hu:Chaconne]] 
[[it:Ciaccona]] 
[[ja:シャコンヌ]]
[[pl:Chaconne]]
[[sv:Chaconne]]

[[Category:Musical forms]]
[[Category:Historical dance]]</text>
    </revision>
